---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Homelab Infrastructure | Etter Digital"
  description="A self-hosted Linux environment using Raspberry Pi, Cloudflare, Tailscale, PM2, PostgreSQL, monitoring, and remote administration."
  type="article"
>
  <!-- HERO -->

  <section class="case-hero">
    <div class="hero-orb hero-orb-one"></div>
    <div class="hero-orb hero-orb-two"></div>

    <div class="container case-hero-grid">
      <div class="case-hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">
          Infrastructure Case Study / Homelab
        </p>

        <div class="project-state-row">
          <span class="live-state">
            <i></i>
            Active / Operational
          </span>

          <span class="mono">
            LINUX / SELF-HOSTING / NETWORKING / OPERATIONS
          </span>
        </div>

        <h1>
          The infrastructure
          <br />
          behind
          <br />
          <span>the projects.</span>
        </h1>

        <p class="hero-description">
          My homelab is the self-hosted environment behind Central Hub,
          Aesthetic King, monitoring services, PostgreSQL, and remote
          administration. It combines Linux, Raspberry Pi hosting,
          Cloudflare, Tailscale, PM2, and network infrastructure into one
          continuously evolving system.
        </p>

        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a
            class="primary-action"
            href="https://dashboard.etterdigital.dev"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            View Live Dashboard
            <span>↗</span>
          </a>

          <a class="secondary-action" href="/#infrastructure">
            Back to Infrastructure
            <span>←</span>
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-stack">
          <span>Raspberry Pi</span>
          <span>Linux</span>
          <span>Cloudflare</span>
          <span>Tailscale</span>
          <span>PM2</span>
          <span>PostgreSQL</span>
          <span>SSH</span>
          <span>Ubiquiti</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-panel">
        <div class="panel-topbar">
          <div class="panel-dots" aria-hidden="true">
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
          </div>

          <span class="mono">
            HOMELAB / TOPOLOGY
          </span>

          <span class="panel-status mono">
            <i></i>
            ONLINE
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-topology">
          <div class="hero-node">
            <span class="hero-node-label mono">PUBLIC EDGE</span>
            <strong>Internet / Cloudflare</strong>
          </div>

          <div class="hero-line">
            <span></span>
          </div>

          <div class="hero-node">
            <span class="hero-node-label mono">NETWORK</span>
            <strong>Ubiquiti / Home Network</strong>
          </div>

          <div class="hero-line">
            <span></span>
          </div>

          <div class="hero-node host-node">
            <span class="hero-node-label mono">PRIMARY HOST</span>
            <strong>Raspberry Pi / Linux</strong>
            <small>ARM64 / Always On</small>
          </div>

          <div class="hero-services">
            <span>Central Hub</span>
            <span>Central Hub Monitor</span>
            <span>Aesthetic King</span>
            <span>PostgreSQL</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container hero-meta">
      <span class="mono">
        HOST / RASPBERRY PI
      </span>

      <span class="mono">
        REMOTE / SSH + TAILSCALE
      </span>

      <span class="mono">
        PROCESS / PM2
      </span>

      <span class="mono">
        DATABASE / POSTGRESQL
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENVIRONMENT -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          01 / Environment
        </p>

        <h2>
          One host.
          <br />
          <span>Several responsibilities.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          The Raspberry Pi acts as the primary application host for my
          self-hosted environment. It runs Linux and supports several
          production services that are exposed publicly, monitored locally,
          or administered remotely.
        </p>

        <p>
          Rather than treating each project as an isolated application, the
          environment is operated as a shared platform with networking,
          process management, persistence, monitoring, and remote access
          responsibilities.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container overview-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="mono">01</span>
        <strong>Application Hosting</strong>
        <p>
          Central Hub and Aesthetic King run continuously from the same Linux
          host.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">02</span>
        <strong>Process Management</strong>
        <p>
          PM2 manages production Node.js processes and supports automatic
          startup after host reboots.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">03</span>
        <strong>Persistent Data</strong>
        <p>
          PostgreSQL provides the production database layer used by projects
          running on the Pi.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">04</span>
        <strong>Remote Operations</strong>
        <p>
          SSH access is available from Windows Terminal and from mobile using
          Termius over Tailscale.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PHYSICAL NETWORK -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          02 / Physical Network
        </p>

        <h2>
          From the ISP
          <br />
          <span>to the Pi.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          The physical side of the environment begins with the ISP connection
          and routes through Ubiquiti networking equipment before reaching
          devices on the home network, including the Raspberry Pi.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container topology-shell">
      <div class="topology-grid" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="topology-flow">
        <article class="topology-node">
          <span class="node-index mono">01</span>
          <span class="node-symbol">ISP</span>

          <div>
            <small class="mono">
              WAN
            </small>

            <strong>
              Internet Service Provider
            </strong>
          </div>
        </article>

        <div class="topology-line">
          <span></span>
        </div>

        <article class="topology-node">
          <span class="node-index mono">02</span>
          <span class="node-symbol">UB</span>

          <div>
            <small class="mono">
              ROUTING / NETWORK EDGE
            </small>

            <strong>
              Ubiquiti Equipment
            </strong>
          </div>
        </article>

        <div class="topology-line">
          <span></span>
        </div>

        <article class="topology-node">
          <span class="node-index mono">03</span>
          <span class="node-symbol">LAN</span>

          <div>
            <small class="mono">
              LOCAL NETWORK
            </small>

            <strong>
              Ethernet / Wi-Fi
            </strong>
          </div>
        </article>

        <div class="topology-line">
          <span></span>
        </div>

        <article class="topology-node host-highlight">
          <span class="node-index mono">04</span>
          <span class="node-symbol">π</span>

          <div>
            <small class="mono">
              APPLICATION HOST
            </small>

            <strong>
              Raspberry Pi
            </strong>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- RASPBERRY PI -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          03 / Raspberry Pi Host
        </p>

        <h2>
          Small hardware.
          <br />
          <span>Real workloads.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          The Raspberry Pi is the primary always-on Linux host for the
          environment. It supports applications, monitoring processes, and
          the production PostgreSQL database while remaining lightweight
          enough for continuous operation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container host-grid">
      <article class="host-card">
        <span class="mono">
          PLATFORM
        </span>

        <h3>
          Linux / ARM64
        </h3>

        <p>
          Provides the operating environment for deployed services and remote
          administration.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="host-card">
        <span class="mono">
          AVAILABILITY
        </span>

        <h3>
          Always On
        </h3>

        <p>
          The Pi operates as a persistent server rather than a temporary
          development machine.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="host-card">
        <span class="mono">
          RESPONSIBILITY
        </span>

        <h3>
          Shared Host
        </h3>

        <p>
          Multiple services run on the same host and must coexist within the
          available compute and memory resources.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PUBLIC ACCESS -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          04 / Public Access
        </p>

        <h2>
          Public services
          <br />
          <span>without direct exposure.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          Cloudflare is used to make selected local web services accessible
          through public domains and subdomains. Visitors reach the public
          hostname while the underlying application continues to run on the
          local Raspberry Pi.
        </p>

        <p>
          This allows services such as Central Hub to be publicly visible
          without relying on direct public access to the local service
          address.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container access-flow">
      <article>
        <span class="mono">
          PUBLIC USER
        </span>

        <strong>
          Browser
        </strong>
      </article>

      <span class="access-arrow">
        →
      </span>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">
          PUBLIC EDGE
        </span>

        <strong>
          Cloudflare
        </strong>
      </article>

      <span class="access-arrow">
        →
      </span>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">
          LOCAL SERVICE
        </span>

        <strong>
          Raspberry Pi
        </strong>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- REMOTE ADMIN -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          05 / Remote Administration
        </p>

        <h2>
          Manage it
          <br />
          <span>from anywhere.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          Remote administration is handled through SSH. From a Windows
          computer I connect using the terminal, while mobile administration
          uses Termius together with Tailscale.
        </p>

        <p>
          Tailscale provides a private network path for remote access, keeping
          administrative connectivity separate from the public application
          endpoints exposed through Cloudflare.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container remote-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="remote-icon">PC</span>

        <div>
          <span class="mono">
            DESKTOP
          </span>

          <h3>
            Windows Terminal
          </h3>

          <p>
            SSH access from a Windows workstation for routine administration,
            deployment, and troubleshooting.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="remote-icon">M</span>

        <div>
          <span class="mono">
            MOBILE
          </span>

          <h3>
            Termius + Tailscale
          </h3>

          <p>
            Mobile SSH access through a private Tailscale connection for
            remote administration when away from a computer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PM2 -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          06 / Application Processes
        </p>

        <h2>
          Services survive
          <br />
          <span>the terminal session.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          PM2 is used to manage the primary application processes running on
          the Pi. This separates application availability from an active SSH
          session and allows services to restart automatically after host
          reboots.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container process-shell">
      <div class="process-header">
        <span class="mono">
          PM2 / PRODUCTION PROCESSES
        </span>

        <span class="process-online mono">
          <i></i>
          MANAGED
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="process-table">
        <div class="process-row process-heading mono">
          <span>PROCESS</span>
          <span>ROLE</span>
          <span>STATE</span>
        </div>

        <div class="process-row">
          <strong>Central Hub</strong>
          <span>Web application</span>
          <span class="process-state">
            <i></i>
            Online
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="process-row">
          <strong>Central Hub Monitor</strong>
          <span>System monitoring</span>
          <span class="process-state">
            <i></i>
            Online
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="process-row">
          <strong>Aesthetic King</strong>
          <span>Discord application</span>
          <span class="process-state">
            <i></i>
            Online
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- POSTGRES -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          07 / Data Layer
        </p>

        <h2>
          Production data
          <br />
          <span>on the host.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          PostgreSQL runs directly on the Raspberry Pi and provides the
          production data layer for applications that require persistence,
          including Central Hub.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hosting the database alongside the applications keeps the current
          environment simple, but also makes backup and recovery planning an
          important next step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container database-flow">
      <article>
        <span class="mono">
          APPLICATION
        </span>

        <strong>
          Central Hub
        </strong>
      </article>

      <span>
        ↔
      </span>

      <article class="database-card">
        <span class="mono">
          DATABASE
        </span>

        <strong>
          PostgreSQL
        </strong>

        <small>
          Raspberry Pi / Production
        </small>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MONITORING -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          08 / Monitoring
        </p>

        <h2>
          Know what
          <br />
          <span>the host is doing.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          Central Hub Monitor provides host and service information used by
          the dashboard. Monitoring turns the Raspberry Pi from a black box
          into an observable system with information about its operational
          state.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container monitoring-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="mono">CPU</span>
        <strong>Usage</strong>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">MEMORY</span>
        <strong>Consumption</strong>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">DISK</span>
        <strong>Capacity</strong>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">TEMP</span>
        <strong>Thermals</strong>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">UPTIME</span>
        <strong>Availability</strong>
      </article>

      <article>
        <span class="mono">SERVICES</span>
        <strong>Process State</strong>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECURITY BOUNDARIES -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          09 / Security Boundaries
        </p>

        <h2>
          Public visibility.
          <br />
          <span>Protected control.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          The environment separates public application access from
          administrative control. Visitors can reach intentionally published
          services, while SSH, editing capabilities, credentials, and
          infrastructure administration remain protected.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container security-grid">
      <article>
        <div class="security-heading">
          <span class="security-icon">◎</span>

          <div>
            <span class="mono">
              PUBLIC
            </span>

            <h3>
              Visitor Access
            </h3>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li>Public websites and dashboards</li>
          <li>Read-only Central Hub access</li>
          <li>Cloudflare-managed public routes</li>
          <li>Selected operational information</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="protected-card">
        <div class="security-heading">
          <span class="security-icon">◇</span>

          <div>
            <span class="mono">
              PROTECTED
            </span>

            <h3>
              Administrative Access
            </h3>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li>SSH administration</li>
          <li>Tailscale remote connectivity</li>
          <li>GitHub-authenticated editing</li>
          <li>Credentials and secrets</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- LIMITATIONS -->

  <section class="case-section limitation-section">
    <div class="container limitation-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          10 / Current Limitations
        </p>

        <h2>
          It works.
          <br />
          <span>It is not finished.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="limitation-copy">
        <p>
          The current environment is intentionally presented as it actually
          exists today rather than as a finished enterprise architecture.
        </p>

        <div class="limitation-list">
          <article>
            <span class="mono">
              01
            </span>

            <div>
              <strong>
                No automated backups
              </strong>

              <p>
                PostgreSQL and other critical application data do not yet
                have a scheduled backup and retention system.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span class="mono">
              02
            </span>

            <div>
              <strong>
                Single primary host
              </strong>

              <p>
                Multiple services depend on one Raspberry Pi, creating a
                single point of failure for the current environment.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article>
            <span class="mono">
              03
            </span>

            <div>
              <strong>
                Limited hardware resources
              </strong>

              <p>
                Services must be selected and operated with the Raspberry Pi's
                available compute and memory resources in mind.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FUTURE -->

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="container future-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          11 / Next Improvements
        </p>

        <h2>
          Build the
          <br />
          <span>operations layer next.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="future-copy">
        <p>
          The next phase of the environment is less about adding random
          services and more about improving reliability, observability,
          documentation, and recoverability.
        </p>

        <div class="future-tags">
          <span>PostgreSQL backups</span>
          <span>Off-device backup storage</span>
          <span>Restore testing</span>
          <span>Resource alerts</span>
          <span>Historical metrics</span>
          <span>Service health checks</span>
          <span>Infrastructure documentation</span>
          <span>Network segmentation</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->

  <section class="case-cta">
    <div class="container case-cta-inner">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">
          Live Infrastructure
        </p>

        <h2>
          See what the
          <span>environment supports.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <a
        class="primary-action"
        href="https://dashboard.etterdigital.dev"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        Open Central Hub
        <span>↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case-hero {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 7rem 0 3rem;
  }

  .hero-orb {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(100px);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero-orb-one {
    top: 3rem;
    left: 14%;
    width: 34rem;
    height: 34rem;
    background: rgba(91, 67, 220, 0.11);
  }

  .hero-orb-two {
    right: -10rem;
    top: 15rem;
    width: 32rem;
    height: 32rem;
    background: rgba(54, 101, 225, 0.07);
  }

  .case-hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 0.95fr 1.05fr;
    gap: 6rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .project-state-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .project-state-row > .mono {
    color: #625c74;
    font-size: 0.52rem;
  }

  .live-state {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.65rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(62, 240, 143, 0.18);
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: #81c99e;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.5rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .live-state i,
  .panel-status i,
  .process-online i,
  .process-state i {
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--status-online);
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(62, 240, 143, 0.5);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(4.3rem, 7.6vw, 8rem);
    line-height: 0.86;
    letter-spacing: -0.075em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 span {
    color: #7d6ca2;
  }

  .hero-description {
    max-width: 690px;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.8rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .primary-action,
  .secondary-action {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.2rem;
    border-radius: 9px;
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.055em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transition:
      transform 180ms ease,
      border-color 180ms ease,
      background 180ms ease;
  }

  .primary-action {
    border: 1px solid rgba(124, 92, 255, 0.72);
    background: rgba(124, 92, 255, 0.12);
  }

  .secondary-action {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .primary-action:hover,
  .secondary-action:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    border-color: rgba(124, 92, 255, 0.7);
    color: white;
  }

  .hero-stack,
  .future-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.45rem;
  }

  .hero-stack {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .hero-stack span,
  .future-tags span {
    padding: 0.42rem 0.6rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(124, 92, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 5px;
